---
import { base } from "../lib/base";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---
<Layout title="The Well-Being Lab | Todd Kashdan" current="well-being-lab">
  <div class="wrap page-hero" style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1.2fr .9fr; gap:44px; align-items:center;">
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Over 25 years of peer-reviewed research</p>
      <h1>An incubator of science on how people can live <em>better</em>.</h1>
      <p class="lead">The Well-Being Lab creates research for public consumption that directly applies to daily life and the workplace. Our focus: emotional agility, resilience after adversity, and the psychological factors that make well-being more likely, for individuals and society.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <img src={base + "images/img_025.jpeg"} alt="Purpose: a central, self-organizing life aim. Strength, Scope, and Awareness, by Drs. Todd Kashdan and Patrick McKnight" style="border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 30px 70px rgba(0,0,0,.55);" />
      <p style="font-size:13.5px; color:var(--bone); margin-top:12px; text-align:center;"><a href={base + "Kashdan-et-al-Purpose-AP.pdf"} target="_blank" style="color:var(--gold); font-weight:700;">Get the articles on our purpose model &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="strip wrap">
    <div><div class="n">250+</div><div class="l">Peer-reviewed articles published and counting</div></div>
    <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=rC3rn04AAAAJ&hl=en&oi=ao" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><div class="n">61,000+</div><div class="l">Citations on Google Scholar &rarr;</div></a>
    <div><div class="n">25+ Years</div><div class="l">Conducting basic and applied research</div></div>
    <div><div class="n">Free Access</div><div class="l">Every research article is available upon request</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="section wrap">
    <div class="sec-h"><h2>Our goals</h2></div>
    <p class="lead" style="max-width:72ch;">High-quality research is about asking interesting questions that get insufficient attention, tested with the best methods possible. Our team has built new conceptual models of curiosity, purpose in life, and psychological flexibility, and designed widely used multidimensional measures of these strengths, shifting the emphasis from feeling good to doing good.</p>
    <ul class="panel" style="list-style:none; padding:26px 30px; margin-top:24px; display:grid; gap:14px;">
      <li>&middot; Better understand and facilitate innovation, and civil, open-minded discourse in groups</li>
      <li>&middot; Understand how curiosity can be harnessed to improve productivity, creativity, and team performance</li>
      <li>&middot; Improve our understanding of well-being and resilience, including how to respond more effectively to positive and negative life events</li>
      <li>&middot; Cultivate psychological strengths, at any age</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="section wrap">
    <div class="sec-h"><h2>Research areas</h2><span style="font-size:14px; color:var(--dim);">Essential articles per area</span></div>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>Curiosity &amp; Creativity</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>The Five-Dimensional Curiosity Scale Revised (5DCR): briefer subscales, splitting overt and covert social curiosity</li>
          <li>Curiosity has comprehensive benefits in the workplace: a multidimensional workplace curiosity scale in U.S. and German employees</li>
          <li>Interesting things and curious people: exploration and engagement as transient states and enduring strengths</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>Meaning &amp; Purpose in Life</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Purpose in life as a system that creates and sustains health and well-being: an integrative, testable theory</li>
          <li>Commitment to a purpose in life: an antidote to the suffering of individuals with social anxiety disorder</li>
          <li>Purpose in life under adversity: Reflections on purpose as a buffer, source, constraint, and target</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>Psychological Flexibility</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Understanding psychological flexibility: a multi-method exploration of pursuing valued goals despite the presence of distress</li>
          <li>Psychological flexibility: what we know, what we don't know, and what we think we know</li>
          <li>Psychological flexibility as a fundamental aspect of health</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>Well-Being &amp; Resilience</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>The curious neglect of high functioning after psychopathology: the case of depression</li>
          <li>Different types of well-being? A cross-cultural examination of hedonic and eudaimonic well-being</li>
          <li>Reconsidering happiness: the costs of distinguishing between hedonics and eudaimonia</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section wrap">
    <img src={base + "images/img_026.jpeg"} alt="The Five Dimensions of Curiosity: Joyous Exploration, Deprivation Sensitivity, Stress Tolerance, Social Curiosity, and Thrill Seeking. Sketchnote citing Kashdan et al. (2020), Personality and Individual Differences, 157:109836" style="border-radius:14px; max-width:480px; width:100%; display:block; margin:0 auto; box-shadow:0 20px 50px rgba(0,0,0,.4);" />
  </div>

  <div class="section wrap">
    <div class="sec-h"><h2>Measures created by our team</h2></div>
    <p class="lead" style="max-width:70ch;">All measures below are available for use in science or practice. But if you are using it for commercial purposes, contact us.</p>
    <div class="grid2" style="margin-top:24px;">
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>The Five-Dimensional Curiosity Scale Revised (5DCR)</h3>
        <p>Kashdan, Disabato, Goodman, &amp; McKnight (2021), <em>Personality and Individual Differences</em>. Stop using the 5DC &mdash; the revised scale has stronger psychometrics, shorter subscales, and splits social curiosity into overt and covert dimensions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>Personalized Psychological Flexibility Index</h3>
        <p>Kashdan, Disabato, Goodman, Doorley, &amp; McKnight (2020), <em>Psychological Assessment</em>. A multimethod exploration of pursuing valued goals despite the presence of distress.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>The M-Workplace Curiosity Scale</h3>
        <p>Kashdan, Goodman, Disabato, McKnight, Kelso, &amp; Naughton (2021), <em>Personality and Individual Differences</em>. A multidimensional workplace curiosity scale, validated in U.S. and German employees.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <h3>The Affective Style Questionnaire</h3>
        <p>Habitual attempts to conceal or suppress affect (Concealing subscale), a general ability to manage, adjust, and work with emotions as needed (Adjusting subscale), and an accepting and tolerant attitude toward emotions (Tolerating subscale).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section wrap">
    <div class="news">
      <div>
        <p class="news-eyebrow">Address</p>
        <h2>Well-Being Lab</h2>
        <p>George Mason University, Mail Stop 3F5, Fairfax, VA 22030</p>
      </div>
      <div class="news-cta">
        <a class="btn solid" href="mailto:todd@toddkashdan.com">Contact the lab</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
